  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Today through Saturday)...\n\nSplitting upper level system moves over the forecast area today \nthrough Monday night. Overall the energy with this system will \nmove north and south of the forecast area, but there will be \nenough residual energy for isolated to scattered showers and a few\nthunderstorms over the area for the next 36 hours. Best chances \nfor precipitation will be over areas east of Billings, and \nespecially along the eastern state line. The Beartooth/Absaroka \nmountains and far SE Montana both show a 40-60% chance for seeing \nat least a quarter of an inch of precipitation today through \nMonday night. Elsewhere the potential for a tenth of an inch sits \nunder 25%. The combination of southeast low level winds and plains\nmoisture may be enough for a strong thunderstorm or two over SE \nMontana from Sunday night through the day Monday. This is a very \nconditional threat with a significant cap in place through period,\nso currently the SPC outlook is just for general thunderstorms \nduring this period. Something to monitor especially if you have \noutdoor plans.\n\nMountain snow potential continues to diminish with negligible\nprobabilities now for accumulating snow even at the highest\nelevations. So, as we talked about yesterday, this system has\nreally become a little to no impact system for the end of the\nweekend into early next week, due to the upper level split.\nTemperatures will be mainly in the 60s across the area the next\ntwo afternoons thanks to plenty of cloud cover over the region. \n\nTuesday into next weekend continue to trend dry with seasonal\ntemperatures as a fairly zonal flow pattern develops and sticks\naround. High temperatures will stick in the 65-75 degree range\nthrough the period. Cluster analysis in the extended period is\nleaning toward ridging (potentially strong ridging) developing for\nnext weekend into the following week. Looks like a very mild and\ndry Fall week from Tuesday beyond. Chambers",
